---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const gradeCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'grade', label: 'ore grade (g/t)', min: 0.5, max: 20, step: 0.5, value: 3, unit: '' },
    { name: 'tonnes', label: 'deposit size', min: 1, max: 100, step: 1, value: 20, unit: 'Mt' },
    { name: 'price', label: 'metal price', min: 500, max: 4000, step: 50, value: 2000, unit: '$/oz' },
    { name: 'cost', label: 'operating cost', min: 300, max: 2500, step: 50, value: 1100, unit: '$/oz' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'contained metal (Moz)', expr: 'grade*tonnes/31.1', unit: '' },
    { label: 'margin per ounce', expr: 'price-cost', unit: '$' },
    { label: 'project value (bn $)', expr: 'grade*tonnes/31.1*(price-cost)/1000', unit: '' },
  ],
};

const explorationCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Why ore forms', note: 'Hot fluids, cooling magma and circulating groundwater concentrate metals a thousand-fold above background — ore deposits are geology\'s rarest cooking.' },
    { math: '2 · Explore with physics first', note: 'Magnetics, gravity and electrical surveys read the rocks from the air; drilling only confirms what the geophysics suspects.' },
    { math: '3 · Drill, log, assay', note: 'Core samples are assayed (metal content measured) and modelled into a 3D block model — each block gets a grade.' },
    { math: '4 · Reserves need confidence', note: 'Inferred → indicated → measured: reserves are only "reserves" when the geology, grade and economics are proven to regulatory standards.' },
    { math: '5 · Grade is destiny', note: 'Everything downstream — pit size, waste, energy, profit — follows from grams per tonne. Low grade means moving more rock per ounce.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/mining-engineering/foundational/ore-deposits-and-exploration">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Mining engineering starts as geology: metals are only worth mining where nature concentrated
    them into ore — rock containing enough metal per tonne to pay for extraction. The discipline's
    first job is finding those rare places, proving them with drills, and computing whether the
    grade, size and price make a mine.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="ore, grade, drill, prove — the exploration pipeline" widget="equationStep" config={explorationCfg} />

  <h2>Grade is destiny: run the numbers</h2>
  <p>
    Contained metal = grade × tonnage (÷ 31.1 for troy ounces). Profit = contained metal × margin.
    Slide the grade down and watch the project die before the geology does — the mining industry's
    hardest truth is that most deposits are real but uneconomic.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="grade, size, price, cost → the mine's economics" widget="sliderLab" config={gradeCfg} />

  <h2>Exploration is elimination</h2>
  <p>
    For every 1,000 prospects, a handful become mines. Geophysics narrows from the air; geochemical
    soil surveys hint at buried ore; drilling delivers the truth at $100+ per metre. The
    exploration geologist's real skill is deciding where <em>not</em> to drill — capital is the
    scarcest resource in the field.
  </p>

  <h2>Why engineers, not just geologists</h2>
  <p>
    Once the deposit is proven, the questions become engineering: how to extract it safely,
    what to do with the waste rock and tailings, how to power a mine in remote terrain, and how to
    close it responsibly. ABET's criteria pair the geology with safety, environmental and economic
    study for exactly this reason — the mine is a system, not a hole.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ABET mining program criteria; Hartman &amp; Mutmansky, <em>Introductory Mining Engineering</em>; Hustrulid &amp; Kuchta, <em>Open Pit Mine Planning and Design</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
